1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The ATR 72 is equipped with a retractable tricycle landing gear system.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
It consists of a twin-wheel nose gear and two twin-wheel main gear units.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The main landing gear units retract into the fuselage pods.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
While the nose gear retracts forward into the nose section.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Each landing gear is maintained in the retracted position by a mechanical up-lock.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The main landing gear is supported by a structural frame attached to the fuselage.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
It features a trailing arm design and a shock absorber for smooth landings.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The braking system is located on the main wheels and includes anti-skid protection.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
The nose gear is steerable and provides directional control on the ground.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
It also has a centering mechanism to ensure it is aligned before retraction.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The landing gear system is electrically controlled and hydraulically operated.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Hydraulic power is provided by the Green hydraulic system.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The landing gear selector lever on the center pedestal controls the operation.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
A locking mechanism prevents the lever from being moved to UP while on the ground.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
This lock is released when the aircraft is airborne and the shock absorbers extend.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
During retraction, hydraulic pressure is applied to release the down-locks.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Then actuators pull the gear into the bays where they are held by up-locks.

18
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The landing gear doors are mechanically linked and close after the gear is retracted.

19
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,000
For extension, the up-locks are released and hydraulic pressure moves the gear down.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Internal locks within the actuators ensure the gear is locked in the down position.

21
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The position of the gear is indicated by lights on the landing gear control panel.

22
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Three green lights indicate the gear is down and locked.

23
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
A red UNLK light means the gear is in transit or not locked.

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
If the green hydraulic system fails, an emergency extension is available.

25
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The pilot must pull the emergency gear release handle.

26
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,000
This bypasses the normal hydraulics and allows the gear to extend by gravity.

27
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Aerodynamic forces also help the gear to reach the down-locked position.

28
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
The nose wheel steering is also powered by the Green system and controlled by pedals or tiller.

29
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,000
The steering angle is limited depending on the control source used.

30
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The anti-skid system monitors wheel speed to prevent tire lock-up.

31
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
It ensures maximum braking efficiency even on contaminated runways.

32
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Regular maintenance of tires and brake wear indicators is essential.

33
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Visual inspection of the gear during walkaround ensures no leaks or damage.

34
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Proper landing gear operation is critical for flight safety.
